INT. SKERRIGAN POINT LIGHTHOUSE - KEEPER'S KITCHEN - NIGHT

Whitewashed stone. A coal stove. A table with five chairs and a dead man's teacup still half full. The lamp above turns slowly, sweeping the window with light.

INSPECTOR ODILE FARR, 40s, rain-wet coat, stands at the head of the table with a notebook. BARTHOLOMEW "BART" GRIGG, 60, the assistant keeper, grey beard, wrings his cap. SISTER AGATHA, 70, a nun in a black habit, folds her hands. PIPPIN, 10, a boy, sits cross-legged under the table with a toy lighthouse.

ODILE
Who last saw Keeper Thorne alive?

BART
Me. Supper, nine o'clock.

AGATHA
And I brought him tea at ten.

PIPPIN
(under the table)
I saw him at eleven.

All heads turn. Pippin looks up, wide-eyed.

ODILE
Where?

PIPPIN
On the stairs. Going up.

BART
Boy, that's not possible. The door was locked.

The telephone on the wall rings. Everyone freezes. Odile lifts it.

ODILE
Farr.

COMMISSIONER (V.O.)
(phone)
Inspector, we have the autopsy. Thorne died at nine thirty.

ODILE
Then who went up the stairs at eleven?

The lamp overhead stops turning. Light holds on the window, still and white.
